// Solo developer (self-contained research implementation)
//
// ============================================================================
//
// 0. CORE ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLE
// ============================================================================
//
// All modules in this repository implement variations of:
//
//     w_{t+1} = T(w_t, σ_t)
//     y_t     = O(w_t)
//     ℓ_t     = L(y_t)
//
// Where:
//
//   T = deterministic or constrained stochastic contraction operator
//   σ = exogenous or structured input signal
//   O = observation projection (non-causal)
//   L = evaluation functional (non-causal)
//
// STRICT RULE:
//
//   - T is the ONLY causal update channel
//   - O and L are read-only projections
//   - No module is allowed to introduce hidden state coupling
//
// Determinism note:
//
//   "strict determinism" applies to structural execution paths.
//   stochasticity, where present, is explicitly seeded or abstracted.
//
// ============================================================================
//
// 1. MODULE FAMILY OVERVIEW
// ============================================================================
//
// Each file is a different *constraint deformation or execution topology*
// over the same contraction kernel family.
//
// They are NOT separate theories.
// They are different instantiations of the same operator algebra.
//
// ============================================================================
// 2. FILE-BY-FILE PURPOSE MAP
// ============================================================================

/*

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
A) dvsm_contraction_kernel.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Minimal contraction operator implementation.

PURPOSE:
    - Defines the base stochastic contraction map T
    - Implements η-driven convergence dynamics
    - Serves as algebraic root of all DVSM systems

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - adaptive filters
    - stochastic approximation (Robbins–Monro form)
    - gradient-free control updates

IMPORTANCE LEVEL:
    ★ foundational kernel (everything depends on this)

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
B) dvsm_bounded_state_machine.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Finite-bounded contraction system with stability constraints.

PURPOSE:
    - Enforces bounded state evolution
    - Prevents divergence via structural constraints
    - Adds safety envelope around kernel behavior

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - embedded controllers
    - firmware state machines
    - bounded estimators in control systems

KEY FEATURE:
    Explicit stability gating (η bounds / saturation behavior)

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
C) dvsm_bounded_fifo_dynamical_system.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Time-windowed contraction system with memory-limited dynamics.

PURPOSE:
    - Introduces FIFO-constrained temporal state window
    - Approximates delayed observation systems
    - Simulates bounded memory dynamical systems

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - streaming signal processors
    - sensor smoothing pipelines
    - rolling-window estimators

DISTINGUISHING PROPERTY:
    State depends on finite history buffer, not global memory

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
D) stochastic_control_system.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Full control-theoretic DVSM instantiation.

PURPOSE:
    - Implements stochastic control loop interpretation of DVSM
    - Adds input-output feedback structure
    - Encodes control-like semantics on top of contraction kernel

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - adaptive control systems
    - Kalman-like iterative estimators (structural analogy only)
    - reinforcement-free control loops

NOTE:
    Does NOT implement learning — only contraction-based adjustment

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
E) stochastic_control_loop.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Minimal closed-loop execution wrapper.

PURPOSE:
    - Executes T → O → L cycle repeatedly
    - Provides runtime orchestration of DVSM system
    - Keeps causal separation strict

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - embedded runtime loop
    - signal feedback loop controller
    - simulation tick engine

DISTINCTION FROM SYSTEM FILE:
    This is orchestration, not model definition

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
F) inverse_adaptive_filter.rs (ANC SYSTEM)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Inverse system identification via contraction dynamics.

PURPOSE:
    - Implements adaptive cancellation (noise suppression behavior)
    - Models inverse signal convergence via contraction
    - Represents error-minimization without explicit gradient descent

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - active noise cancellation (ANC)
    - echo cancellation systems
    - signal inversion filters

IMPORTANT:
    Behavior emerges from contraction, not optimization primitives

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
G) compression_rust_intro.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Lossy projection via contraction collapse.

PURPOSE:
    - Demonstrates compression as state contraction
    - Maps high-variance signals into reduced representation
    - Serves as conceptual bridge between DVSM and information reduction

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - feature extraction pipelines
    - quantization systems
    - dimensionality reduction (nonlinear, implicit)

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
H) shared_state.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Cross-system state representation container.

PURPOSE:
    - Defines shared immutable or semi-structured state objects
    - Enables multi-module interoperability without coupling dynamics
    - Acts as neutral data substrate

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - message buses
    - shared memory buffers
    - serialized state snapshots

IMPORTANT RULE:
    Shared state is NEVER causal — only transportable

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
I) variable_state_v1_v2.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Evolution of state representation formats.

PURPOSE:
    - Demonstrates schema evolution across DVSM versions
    - Encodes versioned state interpretation layers
    - Provides backward-compatible interpretation logic

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - protocol versioning
    - telemetry schema evolution
    - backward-compatible system state encoding

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
J) eil_hardened.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Hardening layer for deterministic execution constraints.

PURPOSE:
    - Enforces strict determinism constraints
    - Removes implicit stochastic ambiguity
    - Stabilizes execution semantics under formal reduction rules

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - safety-critical runtime systems
    - formal verification scaffolds
    - deterministic simulation kernels

IMPORTANT:
    This file does NOT define a layer — it enforces invariants globally

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
K) stochastic_contraction_system.rs
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE ROLE:
    Generalized stochastic contraction framework.

PURPOSE:
    - Unified abstraction over all DVSM contraction variants
    - Encodes parameterized contraction geometry
    - Serves as theoretical superset of kernel implementations

REAL-WORLD ANALOGS:
    - stochastic dynamical systems
    - nonlinear control systems
    - adaptive estimation frameworks

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

3. CROSS-FILE ARCHITECTURAL RULES
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

✔ All systems reduce to contraction dynamics (T)
✔ O and L are observational only (non-causal)
✔ No file is allowed to introduce hidden feedback paths
✔ Shared state is non-causal and transport-only
✔ Versioned state is representational, not ontological
✔ Determinism refers to execution path stability, not absence of noise

// ============================================================================
// 4. DETERMINISM CLAUSE (REFINED)
// ============================================================================
//
// Determinism is NOT an intrinsic property of T alone.
//
// It is a property of the full execution tuple:
//
//     (T, w₀, σₜ)
//
// CASES:
//
// 1) Fully deterministic regime:
//    - σₜ is fixed (or fully seeded)
//    - T is deterministic
//
//    ⇒ identical inputs + identical seeds → identical trajectories
//
// 2) Stochastic regime:
//    - σₜ is stochastic or externally sampled
//
//    ⇒ trajectories are distributionally deterministic,
//       but not path-deterministic
//
// KEY POINT:
//
//   Determinism depends entirely on whether σₜ is:
//
//     • fixed (closed system)
//     • stochastic (open system)
//
// NOT on T alone.
//
// FORMAL SUMMARY:
//
//   T may be deterministic while the system is non-deterministic
//   due to σₜ injection.
//
// ============================================================================
// DVSM — FIXED σₜ DETERMINISTIC EXECUTION EXAMPLE
// ============================================================================

pub trait Operator {
    fn step(&mut self, sigma: f64) -> f64;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Deterministic contraction kernel
// w_{t+1} = w_t + η(σ_t - w_t)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
pub struct DVSMKernel {
    pub w: f64,
    pub eta: f64,
}

impl DVSMKernel {
    pub fn new(w: f64, eta: f64) -> Self {
        Self { w, eta }
    }
}

impl Operator for DVSMKernel {
    fn step(&mut self, sigma: f64) -> f64 {
        self.w = self.w + self.eta * (sigma - self.w);
        self.w
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fixed σₜ generator (deterministic input stream)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
pub struct FixedSigma {
    pub sequence: Vec<f64>,
    pub index: usize,
}

impl FixedSigma {
    pub fn new(sequence: Vec<f64>) -> Self {
        Self { sequence, index: 0 }
    }

    pub fn next(&mut self) -> Option<f64> {
        if self.index >= self.sequence.len() {
            return None;
        }
        let v = self.sequence[self.index];
        self.index += 1;
        Some(v)
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Execution
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn main() {
    // Fixed σₜ (this is what makes the system deterministic)
    let mut sigma_stream = FixedSigma::new(vec![1.0, 0.5, 0.25, 0.75, 1.0]);

    let mut kernel = DVSMKernel::new(0.0, 0.1);

    let mut trace = Vec::new();

    while let Some(sigma_t) = sigma_stream.next() {
        let w = kernel.step(sigma_t);
        trace.push(w);
    }

    println!("trajectory = {:?}", trace);
}

